# Using Loaders and Plugins

Webpack understands JavaScript. A web application is more than JavaScript: it has HTML, CSS, images, and data files. Two mechanisms extend Webpack to cover the rest.

- A **loader** teaches Webpack how to handle one *kind of file* when you import it.
- A **plugin** adds functionality to the build as a whole.

Before either: you do not need to memorize the configuration syntax on this page. Most developers copy an example or use a tool that generates it. What is worth understanding is what each part is *for*, so you can recognize and adjust one when you meet it.

## Loaders

Say you have a stylesheet:

```css
/* src/styles/page.css */
body {
  background-color: black;
  color: white;
}
```

You could link it from your HTML, as we have done since Course 1. With a loader, you can instead import it from JavaScript, next to the code that needs it:

```javascript
// src/index.js
import './styles/page.css';
```

Note the `./`. It is a relative path to your own file, exactly as in Lesson 3, and leaving it off is a mistake that is easy to make and easy to miss.

For that import to work, install two loaders:

```
$ npm install style-loader css-loader --save-dev
```

They do different halves of the job:

- **`css-loader`** reads the `.css` file and turns it into something Webpack can treat as a module.
- **`style-loader`** takes that and injects it into the page at run time, inside a `<style>` element.

Then tell Webpack when to use them:

```javascript
// webpack.config.js
module.exports = {
  entry: './src/index.js',
  module: {
    rules: [
      {
        test: /\.css$/i,
        use: ['style-loader', 'css-loader']
      }
    ]
  }
};
```

Reading the rule:

- **`test`** is a regular expression matched against each file's name. `/\.css$/i` means "ends with `.css`", where `$` anchors it to the end and `i` makes it case insensitive, so `PAGE.CSS` matches too.
- **`use`** lists the loaders to run. The list is applied **from the end backwards**: `css-loader` runs first, because something has to read the file before anything can inject it, and `style-loader` runs on its output. That is why the order looks reversed.

Run `npx webpack` and the page's background is black, from a stylesheet that no HTML file ever mentioned.

CSS is only the common case. There are loaders for images, for data formats, and for translating other languages into JavaScript.

## How a Rule Gets Matched

The matching itself is not complicated, and seeing it run makes the configuration less opaque:

```{javascript autorun}
const rules = [
  { test: /\.css$/i, use: ['style-loader', 'css-loader'] },
  { test: /\.png$/i, use: ['file-loader'] }
];

function loadersFor(fileName) {
  for (const rule of rules) {
    if (rule.test.test(fileName)) {
      return rule.use;
    }
  }

  return ['(handled as JavaScript)'];
}

console.log('page.css  -> ' + loadersFor('page.css'));
console.log('PAGE.CSS  -> ' + loadersFor('PAGE.CSS'));
console.log('logo.png  -> ' + loadersFor('logo.png'));
console.log('index.js  -> ' + loadersFor('index.js'));
```

## Plugins

A loader handles one file at a time. A plugin can do things to the build as a whole.

In the previous readings we wrote an `index.html` by hand with a `<script src="dist/main.js">` tag in it. That is a small piece of duplication waiting to break: rename the output in the configuration and the HTML is silently wrong. **`html-webpack-plugin`** removes it by generating the HTML for you.

```
$ npm install html-webpack-plugin --save-dev
```

```javascript
// webpack.config.js
const HtmlWebpackPlugin = require('html-webpack-plugin');

module.exports = {
  entry: './src/index.js',
  plugins: [
    new HtmlWebpackPlugin()
  ]
};
```

Requiring the plugin and then creating one with `new` is the usual pattern: `plugins` is a list of plugin *instances*, each configured with its own options.

Run the build and `dist/index.html` appears, already containing a script tag pointing at the bundle. Rename the bundle later and the generated HTML follows, because it is generated from the same configuration.

By default the page is otherwise empty, which is rarely what you want. Give the plugin a **template** and it uses your file as the starting point:

```html
<!-- src/index.html -->
<body>
  <h1>Hello, webpack!</h1>
</body>
```

```javascript
  plugins: [
    new HtmlWebpackPlugin({
      template: './src/index.html'
    })
  ]
```

Now `dist/index.html` has your heading *and* the generated script tag.

Templates can also take values from the configuration, using a placeholder syntax:

```html
<title><%= htmlWebpackPlugin.options.title %></title>
```

```javascript
    new HtmlWebpackPlugin({
      template: './src/index.html',
      title: 'Specified in webpack.config.js'
    })
```

That is two of a very large number of loaders and plugins. The ecosystem is most of the reason Webpack is used as widely as it is: whatever kind of file or build step you need, the mechanism for adding it already exists.

## Exercises

1. Add a rule so that any file ending in `.css`, whatever its capitalization, is handled by the two CSS loaders in the working order.

    ```{javascript}
    const rule = {
      // Write the test and use properties here
    };

    console.log(rule);
    ```
    ```+solution
    const rule = {
      test: /\.css$/i,
      use: ['style-loader', 'css-loader']
    };

    console.log(rule);
    ```
    ```+test
    wrap(rule.test instanceof RegExp).should('equal', true).run('`test` should be a regular expression', 'Used a regular expression');
    wrap(rule.test.test('page.css')).should('equal', true).run('It should match `page.css`', 'Matched a css file');
    wrap(rule.test.test('page.CSS')).should('equal', true).run('Add the `i` flag so it matches `page.CSS` too', 'Matched case insensitively');
    wrap(rule.test.test('page.css.js')).should('equal', false).run('Anchor it with `$` so it only matches names that *end* in `.css`', 'Anchored to the end of the name');
    wrap(rule.use).should('equal', ['style-loader', 'css-loader']).run('`use` should list `style-loader` then `css-loader`. The list runs from the end backwards, so `css-loader` reads the file first', 'Listed the loaders in the right order');
    ```

2. Complete `loadersFor` so it returns the loaders configured for a file name.

    ```{javascript}
    const rules = [
      { test: /\.css$/i, use: ['style-loader', 'css-loader'] },
      { test: /\.png$/i, use: ['file-loader'] }
    ];

    function loadersFor(fileName) {
      // TODO: return the use list of the first rule whose test matches fileName

      return ['(handled as JavaScript)'];
    }

    console.log(loadersFor('page.css'));
    ```
    ```+solution
    const rules = [
      { test: /\.css$/i, use: ['style-loader', 'css-loader'] },
      { test: /\.png$/i, use: ['file-loader'] }
    ];

    function loadersFor(fileName) {
      for (const rule of rules) {
        if (rule.test.test(fileName)) {
          return rule.use;
        }
      }

      return ['(handled as JavaScript)'];
    }

    console.log(loadersFor('page.css'));
    ```
    ```+test
    wrap(loadersFor('page.css')).should('equal', ['style-loader', 'css-loader']).run('A `.css` file should get the two CSS loaders. Use `rule.test.test(fileName)` to check a rule', 'Matched the css rule');
    wrap(loadersFor('logo.png')).should('equal', ['file-loader']).run('A `.png` file should get `file-loader`', 'Matched the png rule');
    wrap(loadersFor('index.js')).should('equal', ['(handled as JavaScript)']).run('A file matching no rule should fall through to the default', 'Fell through for JavaScript');
    ```

3. `css-loader` and `style-loader` are both needed. What does each one do?

    ```{mcq}
    Two loaders, two jobs. Which is which?
    correct: Correct! One reads the file so Webpack can treat it as a module, and the other puts the resulting CSS into the page at run time. Neither does the other's job, which is why you install both.
    wrong: One of them has to turn a file into something Webpack understands. The other has to get the CSS onto the page.
    [x] `css-loader` lets Webpack read the CSS as a module, and `style-loader` injects it into the page in a `<style>` element
    [ ] `css-loader` makes text bigger and `style-loader` changes colors
    [ ] `style-loader` is for modern browsers and `css-loader` is for older ones
    [ ] They do the same thing, and the second is a backup
    ```

4. What is the difference between a loader and a plugin?

    ```{mcq}
    How do the two mechanisms differ in scope?
    correct: Correct! A loader is attached to a file type and transforms matching files as they are brought in. A plugin acts on the build itself, which is why generating an HTML file is a plugin's job rather than a loader's.
    wrong: Think about what each one is pointed at: a kind of file, or the build as a whole?
    [x] Loaders handle individual file types during bundling; plugins perform wider tasks on the whole build
    [ ] Loaders are for small files and plugins are for large ones
    [ ] Loaders are free and plugins are paid
    [ ] Loaders are written in JavaScript and plugins in HTML
    ```

